p.src:
The next step is national regulation under the New Deal. In 1934 the United States created the Securities and Exchange Commission. The lecturer does not present it merely as another agency on a list. He makes it vivid through figures such as William O. Douglas and Arthur Levitt, each of whom, in his own way, confronted Wall Street’s hostility to disclosure-based regulation. The guiding principle comes from Louis Brandeis: sunshine is the best disinfectant. In finance that means the following: $\begin{aligned}
\text{secrecy}
\Rightarrow
\text{scope for abuse},
\qquad
\text{disclosure}
\Rightarrow
\text{public scrutiny}
\Rightarrow
\text{cleaner markets}.\end{aligned}$

